; 34-alias.kurt

; `alias NEW OLD` gives an existing symbol an additional name -- most often
; used to offer a nicer Unicode spelling next to an ASCII one.  The core
; (`minimal.kurt`) has no aliases; the loaded theories add them, e.g.
; `propositional.kurt` has `alias ⇒ implies`, so after `load propositional` the symbol `implies`
; also answers to `⇒` (try `parse A ⇒ B` after declaring `prop A, B`), and
; likewise `¬` to `not` and `∧` to `and`.

infix "xor" 15 15
alias "^" xor          ; `^` is now just another name for `xor`

; `A ^ B` below is logged as a restatement of the axiom: an alias is resolved to its
; canonical name while scanning, before parsing even starts, so `A ^ B` is literally the
; exact same formula as `A xor B`. `parse
; A ^ B` would still *display* `(^ A B)`, not `(xor A B)` -- display prefers whatever
; spelling you actually typed -- but only the underlying symbol is what actually gets
; unified/matched.
prop A, B
use A xor B
A ^ B                  ; same formula, either spelling

; An alias is resolved while scanning, before parsing even starts -- unlike
; `def` (12-def.kurt), which introduces a genuinely new constant and needs an
; inference step to unfold (see `doc/dev-notes.md`'s 2025-04-17 notes).

; In Kurt's own theories this is the rule: the standard name of every symbol is an ASCII word,
; and the Unicode symbol is its alias -- set.kurt has `infix union 30 30` and `alias ∪ union`,
; `in` and `∈`, `subset` and `⊂`, `emptyset` and `∅` (the whole table: doc/kurt-ref.md §4.7). A
; proof may write either, and each line is printed as it was written. (Your own files may declare
; Unicode symbols as they like.)
load set
const M, N
use M ⊂ N "M-in-N"
M subset N             ; the same formula, printed with the word
M ⊂ N                  ; ... and with the symbol
